 #### Acceptance Criteria
 
-- User can authorize an Agent Inbox folder through an explicit Settings action that opens a normal Android folder picker. On devices where Google Drive is available as a DocumentsProvider, the user should choose the Google Drive folder there rather than pasting a folder id.
+- User can authorize an Agent Inbox folder through an explicit Settings action that opens a normal Android folder picker. On devices where Google Drive is available as a DocumentsProvider, the user should choose the Google Drive folder there first; pasting a folder id/link remains a recovery path when the system provider or grant cannot expose package children.
 - The Agent Inbox uses the narrowest practical Drive access model and must not scan the user's whole Drive.
-- Under `drive.file`, Agent Inbox folder access must be granted by an explicit Google Picker folder selection or by files the app created itself. Packages uploaded later by a user-controlled external tool such as rclone must be supported only after the selected folder grant is proven to expose those package children to the app; otherwise the fallback scope decision must be documented before widening access or changing the folder-selection mechanism.
-- If the Google Picker folder grant cannot be obtained or cannot expose later rclone-added package children, the preferred user-facing fallback is Android `OpenDocumentTree`: the user selects the Agent Inbox folder in the system folder picker, the app persists the folder URI read grant, and the app scans only that selected tree. A controlled `drive.readonly` fallback may remain for migration or device-specific recovery only after explicit user consent and only for a user-supplied Agent Inbox folder URL/id. The app must not search or scan the user's whole Drive to discover an inbox folder.
+- Google file Picker is not an accepted Agent Inbox folder selector for externally populated packages. Under `drive.file`, packages uploaded later by a user-controlled external tool such as rclone are not assumed visible unless proven by device evidence.
+- Android `OpenDocumentTree` is the primary user-facing folder selector: the user selects the Agent Inbox folder in the system folder picker, the app persists the folder URI read grant, and local/provider-backed trees scan only that selected tree. When the selected tree is backed by Google Drive and exposes a folder id, the app may request explicit `drive.readonly` consent and scan only that selected folder id through Drive API, because the system tree URI alone may not reliably expose rclone-added package children. A controlled `drive.readonly` folder link/id fallback may remain for migration or device-specific recovery only after explicit user consent. The app must not search or scan the user's whole Drive to discover an inbox folder.
 - The app must not silently create a separate app-owned inbox folder when the user intends to scan an externally populated Agent Inbox folder.
 - Each import candidate is represented by a package containing a manifest plus one Markdown or EPUB content file. Markdown packages may also include bounded sidecar image attachments referenced by that Markdown file; EPUB packages must keep their assets inside the EPUB and may not add package sidecars.
 - The package manifest stores title, topic tags, source label, rights class, optional description, optional priority intent, and enough file identity data to detect duplicates.
@@ -499,7 +499,7 @@ The system must provide account-like portability without requiring a Quality Alt
 - Export/import should preserve behaviorally important state without pretending to provide server-backed identity, cross-device conflict resolution, or guaranteed document-file transfer.
 - Any cloud sync must be explicit, revocable, and scoped to user-owned files. Cloud authorization state must be re-established on each device rather than copied through profile export.
 - Agent Inbox cloud access must remain explicit, revocable, finite, and user-reviewed. The product must not ingest agent-produced Drive content without a visible accept/reject step.
-- Agent Inbox Drive access must prefer user-scoped folder selection over broader Drive scopes. If Google Picker folder selection is unavailable, Android `OpenDocumentTree` is the preferred user-facing fallback because it gives a revocable persisted read grant to the selected folder tree without requiring a pasted Drive id. A move to visible `drive.readonly` UX is allowed only after Picker and Android folder-selection flows are tested and documented as unavailable or insufficient for externally added package children. Any retained `drive.readonly` compatibility path must be explicit, revocable, limited in app behavior to the user-supplied Agent Inbox folder id, and described as broader Drive read authorization.
+- Agent Inbox Drive access must prefer user-scoped folder selection over broader Drive scopes. Android `OpenDocumentTree` is the primary user-facing selector because it gives a revocable persisted read grant to the selected folder tree without requiring a pasted Drive id. Google file Picker is not sufficient for externally populated Agent Inbox folders. Any visible `drive.readonly` path must be explicit, revocable, limited in app behavior to the selected or user-supplied Agent Inbox folder id, and described as broader Drive read authorization.
 - Website matching must remain local-first and user-authored. The app must not collect browsing history as an input or side effect of website target matching.
 
